1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,655
Everyone is safe. That is what families

2
00:00:03,695 --> 00:00:06,556
across America read in their newspapers on

3
00:00:06,596 --> 00:00:09,251
the evening of Monday, April fifteenth

4
00:00:09,291 --> 00:00:12,083
nineteen twelve. By then, the Titanic had

5
00:00:12,123 --> 00:00:14,847
been at the bottom of the North Atlantic

6
00:00:14,887 --> 00:00:16,178
for hours.

7
00:00:17,228 --> 00:00:19,830
In New Haven, Connecticut, the City

8
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:23,001
Edition of the Union put it in the biggest

9
00:00:23,041 --> 00:00:25,115
type it had. Titanic afloat.

10
00:00:25,155 --> 00:00:27,456
No lives lost. Ships to rescue.

11
00:00:27,496 --> 00:00:30,738
Every one of those words was wrong.

12
00:00:31,788 --> 00:00:33,742
Underneath, the details.

13
00:00:33,782 --> 00:00:37,065
Big White Star liner, rammed by iceberg

14
00:00:37,105 --> 00:00:40,221
now heading for Halifax, in tow of the

15
00:00:40,261 --> 00:00:43,046
Virginian. Passengers transferred.

16
00:00:43,086 --> 00:00:45,205
It reads like a near miss.

17
00:00:45,245 --> 00:00:48,362
A damaged ship, a rescue at sea, and a

18
00:00:48,402 --> 00:00:50,082
happy ending.

19
00:00:50,732 --> 00:00:53,667
More than two thousand miles to the west

20
00:00:53,707 --> 00:00:56,496
in Moscow, Idaho, the Daily Star-Mirror

21
00:00:56,536 --> 00:00:59,543
went further. Biggest boat afloat wrecked.

22
00:00:59,583 --> 00:01:01,199
All are saved.

23
00:01:02,249 --> 00:01:04,366
And in the nation's capital, the

24
00:01:04,406 --> 00:01:06,793
Washington Times printed a reason to

25
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:09,489
believe it. Liner Titanic kept afloat by

26
00:01:09,529 --> 00:01:12,320
water-tight compartments, being towed into

27
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:13,735
Halifax, Nova Scotia.

28
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:16,499
Water-tight compartments were the feature

29
00:01:16,539 --> 00:01:18,117
everyone had read about.

30
00:01:18,157 --> 00:01:21,116
If they held, the ship would float.

31
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:24,331
Around the headline, the Times ran

32
00:01:24,371 --> 00:01:26,707
portraits of famous passengers.

33
00:01:26,747 --> 00:01:28,316
Major Archibald Butt.

34
00:01:28,356 --> 00:01:30,232
Colonel Archibald Gracie.

35
00:01:30,272 --> 00:01:31,918
Mrs. John Jacob Astor.

36
00:01:31,958 --> 00:01:35,060
Above their faces, in the biggest type on

37
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:37,052
the page, the word afloat.

38
00:01:37,092 --> 00:01:40,194
For one evening, readers had every reason

39
00:01:40,234 --> 00:01:43,413
to think these people were on their way to

40
00:01:43,453 --> 00:01:44,666
Halifax.

41
00:01:45,316 --> 00:01:47,761
Three newspapers. Three cities.

42
00:01:47,801 --> 00:01:49,283
The same good news.

43
00:01:49,323 --> 00:01:50,886
None of it was true.

44
00:01:50,926 --> 00:01:53,932
So how did so many front pages print a

45
00:01:53,972 --> 00:01:56,096
rescue that never happened?

46
00:01:56,136 --> 00:01:58,340
Who printed the truth first?

47
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:01,466
And what does the record say about that

48
00:02:01,506 --> 00:02:04,512
night? To answer that, we have to read

49
00:02:04,552 --> 00:02:07,878
Monday's papers the way their readers did

50
00:02:07,918 --> 00:02:10,041
one page at a time.

51
00:02:10,791 --> 00:02:14,991
Part one. Monday's front pages.

52
00:02:16,341 --> 00:02:19,247
In New York, the news drew crowds to the

53
00:02:19,287 --> 00:02:21,824
offices of the White Star Line, the

54
00:02:21,864 --> 00:02:24,107
company that owned the Titanic.

55
00:02:24,147 --> 00:02:25,580
Look at the picture.

56
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:27,052
A mounted policeman.

57
00:02:27,092 --> 00:02:28,820
A motor car at the curb.

58
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:31,913
Men in overcoats and bowler hats, crowding

59
00:02:31,953 --> 00:02:34,784
the steps. They had come for one thing.

60
00:02:34,824 --> 00:02:37,730
Names. Who was aboard, and who was safe.

61
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:40,676
This glass negative was made by the Bain

62
00:02:40,716 --> 00:02:43,083
News Service that April.

63
00:02:44,133 --> 00:02:47,227
Not every paper printed good news.

64
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:51,007
The same Monday, New York's Evening World

65
00:02:51,047 --> 00:02:54,326
ran this across the top of its final

66
00:02:54,366 --> 00:02:57,645
edition. Liners take off passengers.

67
00:02:57,685 --> 00:03:00,866
Titanic is reported sinking.

68
00:03:01,916 --> 00:03:04,656
And on the same front page, just below the

69
00:03:04,696 --> 00:03:07,305
masthead: Disabled ship under tow, after

70
00:03:07,345 --> 00:03:08,629
hitting big iceberg.

71
00:03:08,669 --> 00:03:11,078
Sinking, and under tow, side by side.

72
00:03:11,118 --> 00:03:13,792
The editors were printing everything that

73
00:03:13,832 --> 00:03:15,293
reached them.

74
00:03:15,943 --> 00:03:17,899
How did the news reach them?

75
00:03:17,939 --> 00:03:20,324
By wireless. The next morning, the

76
00:03:20,364 --> 00:03:22,962
New-York Tribune printed a map of the

77
00:03:23,002 --> 00:03:25,600
North Atlantic. Here, at Cape Race in

78
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,595
Newfoundland, is the wireless station that

79
00:03:28,635 --> 00:03:31,162
first received news of the accident.

80
00:03:31,202 --> 00:03:33,871
From the ships, to shore stations like

81
00:03:33,911 --> 00:03:36,723
this one, then by telegraph to newsrooms

82
00:03:36,763 --> 00:03:39,576
across the country, every message passed

83
00:03:39,616 --> 00:03:40,930
through many hands.

84
00:03:40,970 --> 00:03:43,925
And on Monday, the messages did not agree.

85
00:03:43,965 --> 00:03:46,920
Some said the passengers had been moved to

86
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:49,629
other ships. Some said the Titanic was

87
00:03:49,669 --> 00:03:52,125
sinking. A paper on deadline had to

88
00:03:52,165 --> 00:03:53,264
choose.

89
00:03:54,014 --> 00:03:57,766
Part two. The ship that was gone.

90
00:03:59,116 --> 00:04:01,440
Here is what had actually happened.

91
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:04,007
On Sunday night, April fourteenth, the

92
00:04:04,047 --> 00:04:06,439
iceberg was sighted at eleven forty.

93
00:04:06,479 --> 00:04:09,142
Less than three hours later, the Titanic

94
00:04:09,182 --> 00:04:11,911
was gone. What was left on the water were

95
00:04:11,951 --> 00:04:14,681
her lifeboats. In this photograph, two of

96
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:17,248
them, low in the water, row toward the

97
00:04:17,288 --> 00:04:19,847
ship that would take them in.

98
00:04:21,197 --> 00:04:23,842
In the morning, the liner Carpathia

99
00:04:23,882 --> 00:04:26,987
reached them. She picked up seven hundred

100
00:04:27,027 --> 00:04:29,595
and six people from the lifeboats.

101
00:04:29,635 --> 00:04:32,767
These are survivors, on her deck.

102
00:04:34,117 --> 00:04:36,611
This is the Carpathia's captain, Arthur

103
00:04:36,651 --> 00:04:39,275
Rostron. When his ship reached the scene

104
00:04:39,315 --> 00:04:41,549
there was no Titanic left to tow to

105
00:04:41,589 --> 00:04:43,758
Halifax. And no other ship had her

106
00:04:43,798 --> 00:04:46,422
passengers. Every survivor of the Titanic

107
00:04:46,462 --> 00:04:48,176
was on board the Carpathia.

108
00:04:48,216 --> 00:04:50,710
But it would take time for the world to

109
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:53,309
learn that. On Monday evening, while the

110
00:04:53,349 --> 00:04:55,908
Union's front page was being read in New

111
00:04:55,948 --> 00:04:58,442
Haven, the people this photograph shows

112
00:04:58,482 --> 00:05:00,251
were still at sea.

113
00:05:01,001 --> 00:05:05,137
Part three. Tuesday's correction.

114
00:05:06,187 --> 00:05:08,814
On Tuesday, the good news collapsed.

115
00:05:08,854 --> 00:05:11,851
The Washington Times, the same paper that

116
00:05:11,891 --> 00:05:14,443
had the Titanic under tow the night

117
00:05:14,483 --> 00:05:16,295
before, now printed this.

118
00:05:16,335 --> 00:05:19,406
Last hope of further rescue vanishes, with

119
00:05:19,446 --> 00:05:22,295
wireless message that the Virginian and

120
00:05:22,335 --> 00:05:24,665
Parisian saved none of Titanic's

121
00:05:24,705 --> 00:05:26,120
passengers.

122
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:29,972
The Virginian was the ship that New Haven

123
00:05:30,012 --> 00:05:32,190
had said was towing the Titanic.

124
00:05:32,230 --> 00:05:33,715
Her captain had wired

125
00:05:33,755 --> 00:05:36,556
We arrived too late to rescue anyone, and

126
00:05:36,596 --> 00:05:38,705
we are proceeding to Liverpool.

127
00:05:38,745 --> 00:05:41,339
And from the wireless station on Sable

128
00:05:41,379 --> 00:05:43,210
Island, about the Parisian

129
00:05:43,250 --> 00:05:46,692
She has no Titanic's passengers on board.

130
00:05:47,342 --> 00:05:49,138
Further down the same page

131
00:05:49,178 --> 00:05:51,859
Line officials give up hope for fourteen

132
00:05:51,899 --> 00:05:54,579
hundred and ninety-two souls on Titanic.

133
00:05:54,619 --> 00:05:57,164
On Monday the line's own officials had

134
00:05:57,204 --> 00:06:00,020
been talking about passengers transferred.

135
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,537
By Tuesday evening, they had given up

136
00:06:02,577 --> 00:06:03,517
hope.

137
00:06:04,167 --> 00:06:06,700
In New York, the Tribune's front page

138
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:09,621
read: Thirteen hundred and forty perish as

139
00:06:09,661 --> 00:06:12,195
Titanic sinks. Only eight hundred and

140
00:06:12,235 --> 00:06:14,837
eighty-six, mostly women and children

141
00:06:14,877 --> 00:06:17,619
rescued. It was closer to the truth than

142
00:06:17,659 --> 00:06:20,763
Monday's papers. It was still wrong.

143
00:06:21,413 --> 00:06:23,621
Next to it, a smaller story.

144
00:06:23,661 --> 00:06:26,270
Crowd besieges White Star office.

145
00:06:26,310 --> 00:06:29,641
Line officials, the paper said, could only

146
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:32,852
tell the people outside that six hundred

147
00:06:32,892 --> 00:06:35,903
and seventy-five of twenty-two hundred

148
00:06:35,943 --> 00:06:37,426
were saved.

149
00:06:38,076 --> 00:06:41,196
And across the country, in Oregon, the

150
00:06:41,236 --> 00:06:44,190
Medford Mail Tribune printed its own

151
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:47,434
number. Fourteen ninety-two lives lost.

152
00:06:47,474 --> 00:06:50,927
Every paper had a different count, and the

153
00:06:50,967 --> 00:06:53,339
numbers kept moving for days.

154
00:06:53,379 --> 00:06:55,252
Zero on Monday evening.

155
00:06:55,292 --> 00:06:58,329
Thirteen hundred and forty on Tuesday

156
00:06:58,369 --> 00:07:01,573
morning. Fourteen ninety-two by Tuesday

157
00:07:01,613 --> 00:07:03,876
afternoon in Oregon.

158
00:07:04,626 --> 00:07:07,424
Part four. The record.

159
00:07:08,774 --> 00:07:10,883
On Thursday, April eighteenth, the

160
00:07:10,923 --> 00:07:13,284
Carpathia landed her passengers in New

161
00:07:13,324 --> 00:07:15,749
York. This is the crowd that waited for

162
00:07:15,789 --> 00:07:18,277
her, in the dark, packed into the street

163
00:07:18,317 --> 00:07:19,668
between the buildings.

164
00:07:19,708 --> 00:07:22,133
For the families here, the count was no

165
00:07:22,173 --> 00:07:24,787
longer a headline. It was a list of names

166
00:07:24,827 --> 00:07:26,691
read out one by one.

167
00:07:27,441 --> 00:07:30,012
No lives lost, the New Haven Union had

168
00:07:30,052 --> 00:07:32,554
printed. The National Archives count

169
00:07:32,594 --> 00:07:34,684
of two thousand two hundred and

170
00:07:34,724 --> 00:07:37,089
twenty-three people aboard, fifteen

171
00:07:37,129 --> 00:07:38,944
hundred and seventeen died.

172
00:07:38,984 --> 00:07:41,508
Seven hundred and six lived.

173
00:07:42,158 --> 00:07:44,978
The papers that Monday were not inventing

174
00:07:45,018 --> 00:07:47,838
a rescue. They printed what came over the

175
00:07:47,878 --> 00:07:50,628
wire, as fast as it came, and on Monday

176
00:07:50,668 --> 00:07:51,954
what came was hope.

177
00:07:51,994 --> 00:07:53,977
By Tuesday, it was the truth.

178
00:07:54,017 --> 00:07:56,558
But for one evening, in New Haven, in

179
00:07:56,598 --> 00:07:59,278
Idaho, in Washington, a front page told

180
00:07:59,318 --> 00:08:01,859
families that everyone they loved had

181
00:08:01,899 --> 00:08:04,510
lived. If you had opened that paper on

182
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:07,231
Monday evening, would you have believed

183
00:08:07,271 --> 00:08:09,824
it? Tell me in the comments.

184
00:08:10,974 --> 00:08:13,451
If you like stories told from the papers

185
00:08:13,491 --> 00:08:15,904
themselves, give this video a like, and

186
00:08:15,944 --> 00:08:18,232
subscribe to As Printed, for what the

187
00:08:18,272 --> 00:08:21,326
papers said, and what the archive says.
